Abstract

This statement establishes the priority of a series of core original assertions derived within the Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe (TDMU) framework. All assertions are unified under the Zeroth Axiom (the Unfolding Principle): in a combinatorial network, local reconnections spontaneously tend to relax the network configuration toward a state of lower stress. A topological knot is a metastable, high-stress braided configuration, and its relaxation manifests as mass deficit and stress release. Gravitation is not an independent force opposing unfolding; rather, it is a collective convergence behavior emerging at the macroscopic scale when a vast number of local reconnections seek their respective optimal unfolding paths. The aggregated topological knots generate macroscopic "tightness" through mutual compression—this is a macroscopic consequence of microscopic unfolding, not an independent directive. The entire dynamics of the universe is a unified unfolding of microscopic local relaxation and macroscopic gravitational convergence.

Specific original assertions include: the emergence and expansion of the universe; the attribution error of the Coulomb barrier and the unique identity of the total compressive binding energy (E_comp); a complete classification of the four nuclear reaction pathways; Path I as a statistical inevitability accumulated over time and Path II as a mechanical inevitability under condition trigger; a three-factor criterion explanation for the iron peak; the checkers-jump path and the fully-connected reaction network model; the geometric first principle of chemical bonding; geometric enforcement of the Pauli exclusion principle via exchange-reconnection parity; a hierarchical seam-line stress concentration criterion for isotope fission thresholds; discrete covariant derivative of the superconducting gauge field and topological inevitability of flux quantization; thermal runaway death mechanism and upper mass limit for solid planets; a continuous topological furnace spectrum for gas planets and stars; the stress-accumulation-trigger-release cycle of pulsars; the mixed Path I/II state and energy lockdown inside black holes; extreme stars as optical disguises of black holes under supersaturated accretion; quasars as imprisoned internal recirculating reconnection storms; regenerative ignition and mixed-state star cycles; and a unified Path I explanation for Solar System anomalies.

1. Theoretical Foundation

Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe (TDMU) is a physical framework that takes a discrete topological network as its first principle. Its core postulates are:

Substrate: The most fundamental layer of the universe is a combinatorial network composed of nodes and directed edges, not embedded in any pre-existing spacetime. The sole dynamics of the network is the local reconnection operation. Along any closed path, the difference between the number of counterclockwise and clockwise edges (topological charge) is conserved under reconnection.

Emergence of the Membrane: When the loop density in a region of the network exceeds a critical value, loops organize into a closed surface—a membrane—through collective reconnection. The birth of the universe is the emergence of a membrane from the substrate.

Matter and Topological Knots: Matter consists of stable topological knots on the membrane. Every topological knot carries a topological defect where the braiding cannot be fully closed. The inertia (macroscopic mass) of a topological knot is determined by the total number of reverse reconnection operations required to disentangle it.

Shared Links and Interactions: The connecting paths established between topological knots via the membrane are called shared links, and they are the sole carriers of interactions.

Stress and Relaxation: The measure of what is released or absorbed during reconfiguration of topological structures is called stress. The release of stress corresponds to "energy" in the Standard Model, but in this framework it is not an independent entity; rather, it is a geometric descriptor of changes in the braided structure.

Reattribution of the Coulomb Barrier: The Standard Model regards the Coulomb barrier as the decisive obstacle to nuclear reactions. This framework judges this to be a systematic attribution error. The sole threshold for Path I (defect-complementary weaving) is the geometric matching of topological defects. The observational effects attributed by the Standard Model to the Coulomb barrier are reinterpreted as the apparent form of the total compressive binding energy (E_comp) under charged conditions. E_comp is the single unified threshold of a topological knot's resistance to external compression; it exists for all particle types, and its sole physical identity is the trigger condition for Path II.

2. The Zeroth Axiom: The Unfolding Principle and Cross-Scale Unification of Gravity

Assertion 1: The Unified Geometric Driver of All Cosmic Dynamics

Microscopic Rule (the sole rule): In the combinatorial network, any local reconnection spontaneously tends to relax the network configuration toward a state of lower stress.

Gravitation is not an independent opposing force: Gravity is a collective convergence behavior emerging at the macroscopic scale when an enormous number of local reconnections seek their respective optimal unfolding paths. In the presence of topological knots, the surrounding shared links, in order to achieve their own local unfolding, find that the statistically most efficient path is to pull the connected topological knots closer together, producing a macroscopic convergent effect. The converged knots generate contact stress through mutual compression—this is a macroscopic consequence of microscopic unfolding, not a "reverse command" independent of unfolding. The strength of the gravitational convergence effect varies naturally with the density of shared links, with no critical on/off switch.

Macroscopic manifestations:

Emergence of the Universe: When the loop density in a region of the substrate exceeds a critical value due to statistical fluctuation, the loops spontaneously organize into a closed membrane through collective reconnection. The critical state before emergence (the super-knot) internally stores a maximum amount of topological stress. When the loop density breaks through the critical value, the super-knot violently unfolds into a membrane. The emergence of the universe is the explosive unfolding of the super-knot into a membrane.

Expansion of the Universe: After the membrane emerges, it inherits a portion of the super-knot's crumpled stress. Expansion is the continuous unfolding of the membrane—residual crumpledness is gradually released, the membrane area constantly increases, and the loop density steadily decreases.

End of the Universe: When the loop density on the membrane drops below a critical value, the membrane enters irreversible structural instability—the ultimate unfolding. Global topological reconnections disintegrate the structure, and the membrane shatters into discrete loops, returning to the substrate.

Nature of Stars: Gravitational convergence pulls topological knots together, and compression generates contact stress. This contact stress provides a continuous trigger condition for both Path I and Path II. The entire evolution of a star is the process by which these locally crumpled structures, under gravitational compression, gradually release stress through nuclear reaction pathways.

Geometric Nature of Mass Deficit: Let the braiding complexities of topological knots A and B be C_A and C_B respectively, and that of their fusion product X be C_X. Topological charge is conserved before and after the reaction, while the complexity satisfies C_X < C_A + C_B. The difference ΔC = (C_A + C_B) - C_X is the geometric equivalent of "mass deficit," which is converted into stress waves on the shared link network.

Unified Definition of All Nuclear Transformation Pathways: All Path I, Path II, Path III, and fission are different routes by which a topological knot relaxes toward a more unfolded configuration. Path I is gentle relaxation (defect complementarity, point-by-point release); Path II is violent relaxation (compression exceeds limit, batch release); Path III is ultimate relaxation (topological charge reduces to zero, complete deconstruction back to the ground state); and fission is passive relaxation (structural fracture caused by seam-line stress exceeding the limit).

3. Geometrization of Fundamental Physics

Assertion 2: Fermion Exchange Statistics and Geometric Enforcement of the Pauli Exclusion Principle

The exchange operation of two identical topological knots necessarily corresponds to an odd number of reconnection steps, whereas the identity operation corresponds to zero steps. The opposition in parity forcibly derives the Pauli exclusion principle. Two identical electron bricks cannot occupy exactly the same geometric position and phase state. The shell-filling numbers 2, 8, 18, 32 are geometric outcomes of the discrete winding modes of dangling link ports in three-dimensional space. This derivation uses only the purely combinatorial language of nodes, directed edges, and reconnection step counts, completely eliminating reliance on "antisymmetric wave functions" or the "spin-statistics theorem."

If experiment finds any state in a stable atom where two electron bricks occupy exactly the same spatial winding mode with zero phase difference, this assertion is falsified.

Assertion 3: Hierarchical Seam-Line Stress Concentration Criterion for Isotope Fission Thresholds

The compressive binding energy of an atomic nucleus is decomposed into hierarchically nested quantities: contribution from the dense packing of the inner core, contribution from the outer-shell ports, and the topological stress concentration factor on the transition seam line between the inner core and the outer shell. Chemical properties (outer-shell port orientation) and nuclear stability (seam-line stress concentration) are rigorously decoupled geometrically. When the arrangement of primitive bricks in the inner core contains vacancies, the stress concentration factor is greater than zero; when no vacancy exists, it approaches zero. ²³⁵U has a significantly lower fission threshold than perfectly closed-shell ²³⁸U due to an inner-core vacancy, while the two are completely identical in chemical properties.

If fission thresholds are observed to vary strictly monotonically with neutron number without discrete jumps correlated with shell-closure matching, this assertion is falsified.

Assertion 4: Discrete Covariant Derivative of the Superconducting Gauge Field and Topological Inevitability of Flux Quantization

A discrete covariant derivative is constructed, and the superconducting state is defined as global phase locking. From this definition, the first London equation, the Meissner effect, and flux quantization are directly derived. Flux quantization is an inevitable consequence of closed-path topology—the phase must return to the same discrete count after completing a full loop.

If, in any superconducting system, a magnetic flux is observed that is not an integer multiple of some irreducible elementary unit, this assertion is falsified.

4. Nuclear Reaction Pathways and Elemental Abundances

Assertion 5: Complete Classification of the Four Reaction Pathways

Nuclear reactions between topological knots proceed through four mechanistically distinct pathways, all subsumed under the Unfolding Principle:

Path I (Defect-Complementary Weaving): Defects are geometrically complementary, gently releasing stress. The sole threshold is geometric matching, and the trigger condition is determined by the defect complementarity matching probability of a single collision, independent of any macroscopic critical density. In a fluid medium, there is a negative-feedback self-stabilization loop: local heat release → expansion → density decrease → defect spacing increase → matching probability drop → heat release automatically reduced. In a solid medium, lacking efficient heat dissipation and expansion response, this feedback cannot be established.

Path II (Forced Chain Reconnection): Triggered when external compressive stress surpasses E_comp; connecting edges are cut in batches, leading to large-scale collective reconnection. It does not depend on matching probability; once triggered, it goes to completion inevitably. Light nuclei have low E_comp, heavy nuclei have high E_comp, forming a passive self-stabilizing gradient. E_comp is the single unified threshold of a topological knot's resistance to external compression, and its sole physical identity is the trigger condition for Path II. For charged topological knots at intermediate-to-long distances, the shared-link density gradient dominates, and E_comp manifests as a 1/r decay (corresponding to the Standard Model's Coulomb barrier); at close nuclear distances, the link saturation approaches a maximum, and E_comp manifests as short-range saturation (corresponding to the strong nuclear force); at extreme densities, the saturation rigidity of network connections dominates, and E_comp manifests as incompressibility (corresponding to degeneracy pressure). The three apparent forms share a single origin.

Path III (Topological Dissolution): When the sum of the global topological charges of two topological knots is zero, defect interlocking leads to complete deconstruction; the final state is purely radiation. This is the limiting form of the Unfolding Principle. Path III only blocks specific reaction channels, not all synthesis paths for nuclides of that mass number.

Fission (Stress Tearing): A mechanical tearing that occurs when the stress on the internal seam line of a topological knot exceeds the limit; it is an intrinsic property of the nuclide itself.

Assertion 6: The Axiomatic System of Path I and Path II

Path I is a statistical inevitability accumulated over time: defect matching in a single collision is a probabilistic event, and the probability can be extremely low. However, given the total number of particles, collision frequency, and cosmic timespan at astronomical scales, the law of large numbers locks this extremely low probability into a macroscopically observable statistical certainty. Path I can occur at any density and any temperature; whether it manifests macroscopically as observable "burning" depends on whether the total event rate reaches a significant level—this is a statistical threshold, not a critical condition for mechanism triggering.

Path II is a mechanical inevitability under condition trigger: if the condition is met (external compressive stress exceeds E_comp), it triggers inevitably; if the condition is not met, it does not trigger.

In the core regions of stars where gravitational convergence is sufficient, both inevitabilities hold simultaneously—Path I is locked by statistics, and Path II is triggered by compression.

Assertion 7: Three-Factor Abundance Criterion and Explanation of the Iron Peak

The abundance of any given nuclide is jointly determined by synthesis probability, continued fusion probability, and fission probability. Net retention is proportional to the synthesis probability multiplied by the continued retention rate.

The prominent abundance of iron-56 results from a net accumulation of nucleon flow at mass number 56: the nucleon flow can arrive via both stepwise and checkers-jump paths; the continued fusion probability is low (minimal defect surface area fraction), and the fission probability is low (balanced seam-line stress). The iron peak has no causal relationship with stellar evolution—the special role of the iron core in stellar evolution arises from its extremely high E_comp, which is an independent issue from the cause of the iron peak's abundance.

Assertion 8: The Checkers-Jump Path and the Fully-Connected Reaction Network

There are no mandatory pathways on the nuclide mass spectrum. Any two topological knots can fuse as long as their defects match. Multiple precursor combinations can converge to the same final state. The abundance of a nuclide is the sum of the contributions from all channels in the fully-connected reaction network that can converge to that node. The checkers-jump path and the stepwise path are parallel channels whose weights are dynamically determined by the abundance ratios of raw materials.

5. Geometric Foundation of Chemistry and Materials

Assertion 9: The Geometric First Principle of Chemical Bonding

The entire theory of chemical bonding can be systematically reconstructed using only geometric concepts such as nodes, directed edges, shared links, and dangling link ports:

Covalent Bond: A highly saturated, highly directional local shared link channel. Bond order corresponds to the number of parallel channels. Molecular shape is determined by the spatial orientations of the dangling link ports.

Ionic Bond: A macroscopic manifestation of link density gradient.

Metallic Bond: A delocalized sea of shared links. Ductility arises from the ability of the low-saturation network to undergo local reconnections under shear.

Carbon allotropes are textbook examples of three-dimensional dangling-link port orientation strategies: diamond (4 ports, fully locked in 3D), graphite (3 ports locked in 2D + 1 port weakly bonded), graphene (3 ports locked in 2D + 1 port suspended).

6. Unified Picture of Astrophysical Evolution

Assertion 10: Thermal Runaway Death Line and Upper Mass Limit for Solid Planets

Solid media have extremely slow heat dissipation and limited thermal expansion. Once core Path I enters positive feedback, local stress escalates exponentially, Path II is ignited, and the entire planet is disassembled layer by layer from the core outward. This explains the "radius valley" in the exoplanet radius distribution. The mass upper limit corresponds to the critical point where the ratio of Path I heat release power to lattice thermal conduction power exceeds 1—below this mass, the ratio is <1, and Path I operates steadily; above this mass, the ratio is >1, positive feedback triggers Path II ignition.

If a large number of long-term stable pure solid planets are found in any mass range (with no truncation feature), this assertion is falsified.

Assertion 11: A Continuous Topological Furnace Spectrum for Gas Planets and Stars

Planets are not "failed stars." The Sun, Jupiter, and Saturn constitute a complete spectrum from burning to smoldering, differing only in the macroscopic event rate of core Path I. Using observed celestial heat flux as a constraint to back-calculate the macroscopic effective probability of Path I: the solar core has a probability of about 10⁻²⁹, while the Jupiter-wide weighted probability is about 10⁻³². The difference reflects the systematic influence of the environment on matching probability.

Assertion 12: The Stress-Accumulation-Trigger-Release Cycle of Pulsars

Pulsars are solid, compact topological stars. Internally, Path I and Path II operate continuously. The solid medium lacks the fluid expansion cooling capability, so stress accumulation causes a localized central region to periodically cross the Path II trigger threshold, releasing a shock wave that propagates to the surface and produces a spallation jet, forming the pulse. The period is the time required for stress to accumulate to the trigger threshold. The stability originates from the statistical event rate locked by the law of large numbers. Glitches and magnetars are manifestations of the same mechanism at different scales.

If pulsars, glitches, and magnetars are found to have insurmountable physical boundaries rather than a continuous transition, this assertion is falsified.

Assertion 13: Mixed Path I/II State and Energy Lockdown Inside Black Holes

Black hole-level topological stars are in a globally phase-locked superfluid state. Internally, Path I and Path II necessarily operate simultaneously, but the stress release of both mechanisms is locked down with extremely high efficiency: local production → blocked by shared-link gradient potential wells and the absence of dangling ports → converted into quantized topological vortices, contributing as an increment of effective mass. The "blackness" of a black hole is the result of nuclear reaction energy release being geometrically locked down. The lockdown efficiency is extremely high but not absolute; there exists a very low rate of mass dissipation. Triple geometric confinement ensures that the structure never shatters explosively. The three mass limits—lower limit (superfluid shell closure critical), gap (half-closed phase transition instability region), and upper limit (superfluid phase-locking collapse)—are all explained uniformly by the same set of geometric parameters.

Assertion 14: Extreme Stars as Optical Disguises of Black Holes Under Supersaturated Accretion

Supermassive stars (M > 100 M⊙) and super red supergiants (R > 1500 R⊙) are optical disguises of already-formed black hole-level topological stars under supersaturated accretion conditions. The visible surface is a thick, dense envelope formed by enormous amounts of matter falling toward the black hole and piling up outside the fission surface. The interior of the envelope is a gradient coexistence zone of Path I and Path II, with Path I absolutely dominating the surface layers; its negative-feedback self-stabilization loop determines the observed spectrum and radius. Volume is decoupled from core mass, rendering the mass-luminosity relation invalid. When the envelope is depleted, the object will vanish directly from the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram without undergoing a supernova explosion, naturally resolving the red supergiant paucity problem.

If time-domain monitoring of such objects reveals any explosive behavior consistent with an iron-core-collapse supernova, this assertion is falsified. If for all such stars the ratio of asteroseismic mass to photometric mass is approximately 1, this assertion is falsified.

Assertion 15: Quasars as Imprisoned Internal Recirculating Reconnection Storms

Quasars are primordial topological knot clusters of sufficiently large mass in the very early universe that skip the stellar stage and directly enter an imprisoned internal recirculating Path II reconnection storm. Fission cycles and Path III superimpose within them to contribute to the extreme luminosity. Black hole lockdown and quasar storms are two operating regimes of the same class of topological stars under different matter supply rates: when the external matter supply rate exceeds the mass dissipation rate, the accumulated contact stress forms sustained Path II triggering outside the fission surface, manifesting as a storm; when the supply rate is lower than the dissipation rate, external matter cannot continuously pile up, the core's superfluid shell is exposed, manifesting as lockdown.

Assertion 16: Regenerative Ignition and Mixed-State Star Cycles

Stellar evolution is not a one-way irreversible process. When the core encounters a fusion trap, gravitational compression causes stress accumulation, local Path II is triggered, forcibly disassembling heavy nuclei into light-nucleus fragments that re-enter the Path I raw material pool—this is regenerative ignition. Mixed-state stars are steady-state structures where Path I and Path II coexist in varying proportions, forming a complete continuous spectrum from pure Path I to pure Path II.

Assertion 17: A Unified Path I Explanation for Solar System Anomalies

Numerous mutually independent anomalous phenomena in the Solar System can be attributed to the macroscopic manifestation of Path I in different environments: excess heat flux from Jupiter and Saturn; Earth's plate tectonics; Earth's geomagnetic field and its reversals; Venus's lack of an intrinsic magnetic field; Mars's magnetic field disappearance; endogenous origin of gold; endogenous origin of petroleum; and the physical inevitability of the origin of life.

7. Conceptual Network of the Shared Underlying Postulates

The above seventeen assertions share a single set of underlying postulates and linguistic system, starting from the same set of first principles and covering a complete physical picture from quantum statistics to cosmological scales. The core relational structure is as follows:

Assertion 1 (the Unfolding Principle and cross-scale unification of gravity) is the Zeroth Axiom that governs all physical processes. Gravity is not an adversary of unfolding but the emergent manifestation of microscopic unfolding at macroscopic scales.
Assertions 2 to 4 (Pauli exclusion, isotope fission, superconducting gauge field) bring quantum statistics, nuclear structure, and condensed matter physics into the same geometric framework.
Assertions 5 to 8 (four pathways, pathway axioms, three-factor criterion, checkers-jump path) form the microscopic basis of nuclear reactions and elemental abundances.
Assertion 9 (chemical bond geometry) unifies molecular structure under the spatial orientation strategy of dangling link ports.
Assertions 10 to 17 (astrophysical spectrum) demonstrate the cross-level unification of the Unfolding Principle from planets to the cosmic scale.
These assertions uniformly reject the fundamental attributions of the Standard Model for the relevant phenomena: the Coulomb barrier is judged to be an attribution error; electromagnetism, the strong nuclear force, and degeneracy pressure are unified as the apparent forms of E_comp under different conditions; the mass-luminosity relation is shown to fail under extreme conditions; the "blackness" of black holes is redefined as geometric lockdown; stellar evolution is corrected from one-way irreversible to cyclic regeneration; the birth of the universe is corrected from a Big Bang to emergence.

8. Conclusion

This statement establishes the discovery date and priority of the seventeen core original assertions derived within the Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe framework. All assertions are unified under the Zeroth Axiom: the entire dynamics of the universe is a unified unfolding of microscopic local relaxation and macroscopic gravitational convergence. Gravity is not an opponent of unfolding, but the emergent manifestation of unfolding at the macroscopic scale. The universe was not created—it emerged. Expansion is not driven—it is unfolding.

